{
    "@visokiotype": "CustomBlockSchema.CustomBlockManifest",
    "name": "Price Elasticity",
    "scriptFilename": "script.py",
    "language": "PYTHON",
    "executableVersion": null,
    "minVersions": [
        null
    ],
    "optionsVersion": 1,
    "apiVersion": "VERSION_0",
    "isResourceIntensiveScript": false,
    "showPartitioning": false,
    "icon": "data:image/png;base64,",
    "description": "Estimates price elasticity of demand per product with a log-log regression (optional control drivers), with confidence intervals, revenue impact and demand curves.",
    "category": "Analytics",
    "subcategory": "Pricing",
    "tags": [
        "price elasticity",
        "pricing",
        "demand",
        "regression",
        "revenue management",
        "retail",
        "visokio-agents"
    ],
    "introductoryText": "Estimates how sensitive demand is to price, per product or segment, from historical price and volume observations (for example weekly sales).\n\nThe model is the standard **log-log regression**: log(volume) = a + b x log(price) (+ optional control drivers such as a promotion flag, distribution or seasonal dummies). The **elasticity** b means a 1% price rise changes volume by about b%:\n\n- |b| > 1, **elastic**: a price rise loses more volume than it gains in price, so revenue falls\n- |b| < 1, **inelastic**: a price rise raises revenue\n\nPer product you get the elasticity, its **confidence interval** and **p-value**, R squared, the observed price range, the estimated **volume and revenue effect** of a chosen price change and a one-line interpretation (including a warning when volume appears to rise with price, a sign of confounding). A **demand curve** output gives predicted volume and revenue across the observed price range, and an observations output adds fitted volumes.\n\nEstimates are only as good as the price variation in the data: prices that never change give no estimate, and effects outside the observed price range are extrapolation.",
    "dependencies": "",
